From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: orbis-win <orbis-win@invalid>
Subject: [PATCH] util/log, wsi/orbis: radv.log lines are written by a thread of
 their own (ORBIS_LOG_ASYNC)

Every Mesa log line was an fprintf+fflush to radv.log (MESA_LOG_FILE, on
/data, the HDD the game streams from) on the thread that logged it. In
Psychonauts at a 30 fps lock the only hitch in two minutes indoors was one
155 ms frame, 121 ms of it a single radv.log line written inside
vkQueuePresentKHR.

logger_file now copies each formatted line into an in-memory ring (64 KiB)
under a mutex the writer holds only for a memcpy, and one writer thread
writes the ring to the same FILE, so radv.log's bytes do not change. The
writer has a 64 KiB stack: scePthreadCreate with an explicit attr, which
neither orbis-compat's pthread_create floor (16 MiB) nor the libvulkan
link's scePthreadCreate wrap (keeps a chosen stack up to 4 MiB) raises. The
host build of the orbis arm uses pthread_create with the same attr.

- ERROR lines are still written on the logging thread, after it writes out
  what the ring holds under the writer's I/O mutex: the order in the file is
  kept, and an error is on disk before a crash can lose the ring.
- A full ring drops the line and counts it; the next line that fits follows
  one warning, "mesa log: N line(s) dropped - the async log ring (K KiB) was
  full". Nothing waits for the disk to queue a line.
- A write of 50 ms or more on the writer logs "mesa log: a radv.log write of
  B bytes took N us on the async log writer, off the logging thread", at
  most once a second.
- A crash that logs no ERROR line first can lose what the ring holds: up
  to 64 KiB of the newest INFO and WARN lines.
- ORBIS_LOG_ASYNC=0 (radv.env) writes every line on its own thread as
  before; ORBIS_LOG_ASYNC=N (16..4096) sets the ring in KiB; unset or 1 is
  64 KiB.
- The hung-submit report (ORBIS_DUMP_HUNG, set by DEV_GPU_HANG_REPORT=1)
  keeps every line synchronous for the whole run: its dumps are megabytes of
  INFO lines just before the process dies, which a ring would drop or lose.
- The first swapchain logs "wsi/orbis: async log on (ring 64 KiB) ..." (or
  "async log off ..." when ORBIS_LOG_ASYNC=0 or ORBIS_DUMP_HUNG is on). Swapchain teardown waits up to 250 ms, and atexit up
  to 500 ms, for the ring to empty.
- Not routed: mesa_log_get_file() users (nir_print_shader and other debug
  dumps) still write the FILE directly, unordered against the ring.
---
